::CW_U06_L05_Q01::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] A student is preparing a memoir excerpt for digital publication. Which first step best matches the lesson process?{
=A. Identify the genre and decide what the reader needs to see clearly.#Correct. Publication formatting begins with genre and reader needs.
~B. Add decorative symbols before every paragraph.#Incorrect. Decoration is not the first step and may distract from readability.
~C. Delete the title to save space.#Incorrect. A title helps prepare the reader. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Submit the first draft without previewing it.#Incorrect. A publication-ready file needs a final check.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q02::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which choice best improves readability in a prose scene?{
~A. Put dialogue from three speakers in one paragraph.#Incorrect. This makes the scene difficult to follow.
~B. Remove paragraph breaks because they are optional.#Incorrect. Paragraph breaks guide readers through action and dialogue.
=C. Start a new paragraph when a new speaker talks.#Correct. This helps readers track speaker changes. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Add random blank lines after every word.#Incorrect. Random spacing creates confusion. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q03::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] A poem's line breaks were copied incorrectly into one paragraph. What should the writer do?{
~A. Turn the poem into a script.#Incorrect. The genre does not need to change.
~B. Add unrelated images to fill the page.#Incorrect. Extra content does not solve a formatting problem.
~C. Remove all white space forever.#Incorrect. White space can be meaningful in poetry.
=D. Restore purposeful line breaks and stanza breaks from the revised poem.#Correct. Line and stanza breaks are part of the poem's meaning and pacing.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q04::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which file-readiness action is strongest?{
~A. Keep bracketed notes that say fix later.#Incorrect. Draft notes make the piece look unfinished.
=B. Use a clear file name, preview the file, and remove draft comments.#Correct. These steps prepare the piece for a reader or LMS submission.
~C. Submit several versions so the reader can choose.#Incorrect. The assignment asks for the final version unless drafts are requested.
~D. Ignore the file name.#Incorrect. Clear file naming supports organization and submission.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q05::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] A student says, "I made the font bright because publication should look exciting." What is the best feedback?{
~A. Correct, because bright color always improves writing.#Incorrect. Visual excitement does not automatically improve readability.
~B. Correct, because conventions no longer matter online.#Incorrect. Digital publication still requires readable conventions. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Incorrect, because formatting should support genre, readability, and audience access.#Correct. Publication formatting is purposeful, not random decoration.
~D. Incorrect, because all creative work must use plain text only.#Incorrect. The issue is not all visual choice; the issue is whether the choice supports the reader.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q06::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which question belongs in a digital readability check?{
=A. Does every break, label, or paragraph help the reader follow the piece?#Correct. This directly checks whether formatting choices support readability.
~B. Is the piece impossible to revise?#Incorrect. The check is about reader access, not giving up revision.
~C. Did the writer choose the longest possible title?#Incorrect. A title should be clear, not automatically long.
~D. Can the reader guess missing context?#Incorrect. A reader-ready piece should not rely on guessing.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q07::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] A script draft has stage directions mixed into spoken dialogue. What revision best supports the reader?{
~A. Remove all stage directions from the play.#Incorrect. Stage directions can be useful when clearly formatted.
~B. Add a title but leave the dialogue confusing.#Incorrect. The title alone does not fix the script format problem.
~C. Replace all character names with initials without explanation.#Incorrect. That may make the script harder to follow.
=D. Format stage directions consistently so they do not look like spoken lines.#Correct. This helps the reader distinguish action from dialogue.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q08::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which publication note is strongest?{
~A. I submitted it because I was finished.#Incorrect. This does not explain any format choice.
=B. I used stanza breaks to separate the three image groups so the reader can follow the poem's movement.#Correct. This names the formatting choice and explains reader effect.
~C. I changed things randomly until it looked different.#Incorrect. Publication formatting must be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. I used a bright font because readers like color.#Incorrect. This does not connect format to genre or meaning.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q09::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] What is the purpose of previewing a file before submission?{
~A. To avoid reading the piece again.#Incorrect. Previewing is a form of careful rereading.
~B. To add more draft comments.#Incorrect. Draft comments should usually be removed from the final version.
=C. To see the piece the way a reader will see it.#Correct. Previewing helps catch spacing, format, and readability problems.
~D. To change the genre at the last minute.#Incorrect. Previewing checks presentation, not genre replacement. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q10::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which choice best describes format-specific quality work?{
~A. Every genre uses exactly the same format.#Incorrect. Different genres have different reader needs. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The writer adds as many visual effects as possible.#Incorrect. Effects that distract from reading weaken publication quality.
~C. The file name is left blank.#Incorrect. A clear file name supports readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. The piece is formatted so its genre is clear and readable.#Correct. Format-specific quality means the final form supports the kind of writing.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q11::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] A student includes a note at the top that says "rough version, maybe final." What should happen before submission?{
=A. Remove the draft label and submit a clearly final version.#Correct. The reader should receive the intended final version.
~B. Leave it because it is honest.#Incorrect. A final publication version should not include confusing draft labels.
~C. Add more uncertain notes.#Incorrect. More draft notes make the piece less ready.
~D. Hide the title instead.#Incorrect. The title should stay clear. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q12::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which conventions pass is most appropriate for a final publication version?{
~A. Rewrite the entire piece into a new topic.#Incorrect. A conventions pass is not a full replacement of meaning.
~B. Ignore dialogue punctuation because it is creative writing.#Incorrect. Dialogue punctuation helps readers understand speech. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Check capitalization, punctuation, spelling, and paragraphing without changing intentional style.#Correct. This removes distracting errors while preserving creative choices.
~D. Add misspellings to make the piece unique.#Incorrect. Accidental errors distract readers. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q13::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which answer best explains why a title matters in digital publication?{
~A. A title replaces the need for formatting.#Incorrect. The piece still needs readable format. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. A title can prepare the reader and make the piece look complete.#Correct. A clear title gives the reader an entry point.
~C. A title should always reveal the ending.#Incorrect. Titles do not have to spoil the piece.
~D. A title is only needed for research papers.#Incorrect. Creative writing pieces often need titles too.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q14::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] A writer uses different spacing styles for each character label in a script. What problem does this create?{
~A. It makes the script too poetic.#Incorrect. The problem is not poetry; it is inconsistent readability.
~B. It automatically improves performance.#Incorrect. Inconsistency usually makes performance harder. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. It removes the need for stage directions.#Incorrect. Labels and stage directions serve different purposes.
=D. It can confuse readers about who is speaking and how the scene is organized.#Correct. Consistent labels support script clarity. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q15::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which action best prepares a portfolio piece for online collaborative publication?{
=A. Check that the final version is clear, complete, readable, and named correctly.#Correct. These qualities support online publication readiness. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Submit a version with unresolved comments.#Incorrect. Unresolved comments can confuse readers unless process evidence is requested.
~C. Use unclear labels so readers ask questions.#Incorrect. Publication should reduce unnecessary confusion. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Remove all formatting from every genre.#Incorrect. Genre-specific formatting is part of quality.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q16::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Why should a writer remove placeholder labels such as "fix this later"?{
~A. They prove the writer revised carefully.#Incorrect. Leftover labels suggest the final pass was incomplete.
~B. They are required in every final draft.#Incorrect. Final drafts should usually remove drafting labels.
=C. They make the piece look unfinished and distract the reader.#Correct. Final publication should not include accidental drafting reminders.
~D. They replace the need for a publication note.#Incorrect. A publication note explains choices; placeholders show unfinished work.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q17::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which sentence best distinguishes formatting from decoration?{
~A. Formatting means adding unrelated pictures.#Incorrect. That describes decoration, not necessarily useful format.
=B. Formatting supports readability and genre; decoration may be visual but not always useful.#Correct. Purposeful formatting helps the reader, while decoration can distract.
~C. Decoration always improves publication quality.#Incorrect. Decoration can weaken readability. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Formatting is only about making text smaller.#Incorrect. Formatting includes title, spacing, breaks, labels, and order.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q18::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which example shows a reader-ready opening?{
~A. No title, no author label, and a draft note.#Incorrect. These features make the piece look unfinished.
~B. Random blank lines before the first sentence.#Incorrect. Random spacing can confuse readers. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A warning that the writer did not proofread.#Incorrect. Final publication should be proofread. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. A clear title, clean first paragraph, and no leftover drafting comments.#Correct. These choices prepare the reader to enter the piece.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q19::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] A student says, "I cannot change any spacing because the words are already revised." What is the best response?{
=A. Spacing is part of publication readiness and can be revised without changing meaning.#Correct. Formatting changes can improve reader access while preserving content.
~B. Spacing never matters.#Incorrect. Spacing affects readability, pacing, and genre signals.
~C. Only teachers can decide line breaks.#Incorrect. Writers make purposeful formatting choices. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Publication formatting means rewriting the whole piece.#Incorrect. It often means preparing the existing piece for readers.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q20::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which final check best supports clarity, cohesion, and conventions?{
~A. Only check the last word.#Incorrect. The whole piece needs a final pass.
~B. Trust that spellcheck catches everything.#Incorrect. Spellcheck can miss wrong-word errors. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Read one sentence, line, or script beat at a time to catch distracting errors.#Correct. Slow checking helps identify issues that affect reader understanding.
~D. Add longer words to sound formal.#Incorrect. Longer words do not automatically improve clarity.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q21::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which formatting choice best supports a poem's pacing?{
~A. Break every word into separate letters.#Incorrect. Random breaks distract from meaning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Remove all stanza breaks because poems should be crowded.#Incorrect. Stanza breaks can guide movement. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Use paragraph indentation for every line without purpose.#Incorrect. Format choices should support the poem. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Put an important final phrase on its own line when the pause supports meaning.#Correct. A purposeful line break can slow the reader and emphasize the moment.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q22::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] What should a writer do if the digital preview shows broken spacing?{
~A. Ignore it because the draft looked fine earlier.#Incorrect. The preview shows what the reader may see.
=B. Fix the spacing and preview again before submission.#Correct. This confirms the reader-facing version is ready.
~C. Submit a screenshot instead of the assignment.#Incorrect. The assignment requires the correct final file.
~D. Add a note telling readers to imagine the spacing.#Incorrect. The final version should display correctly. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q23::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which response best explains online collaborative publication?{
=A. Writing is prepared so others can access, read, and respond to it in a shared digital space.#Correct. Online publication requires reader access and readiness.
~B. Writing is hidden so no one can read it.#Incorrect. Publication means the work is made available to readers.
~C. Writing is submitted with no title and no format.#Incorrect. Reader access requires preparation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Writing is changed into a spreadsheet.#Incorrect. The format should fit the genre and assignment.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q24::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which mistake would most likely lower publication quality?{
~A. Using consistent paragraphing in a story.#Incorrect. That supports readability. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Leaving unresolved comments, random spacing, and a missing title in the final file.#Correct. These issues make the piece look unfinished and harder to read.
~C. Previewing the file before submission.#Incorrect. Previewing improves readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Explaining a purposeful formatting choice.#Incorrect. That supports mastery. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L05_Q25::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which final submission choice is best?{
~A. Submit only a rough outline with no title.#Incorrect. The checkpoint requires a publication-ready piece.
~B. Submit every draft without identifying the final version.#Incorrect. The reader needs the intended final version.
=C. Submit the final formatted piece and a note explaining how format supports the reader.#Correct. This shows both publication readiness and craft reasoning.
~D. Submit a file with draft comments still visible.#Incorrect. Draft comments usually need to be removed from final publication.
}
